# Phase 3 — Data Wrangling Methodology (FINAL)

> Records every dataset-wrangling decision with exact numbers, ready to drop
> into the paper's Methodology section and serve as the audit trail for the
> graded wrangling work. Updated to the final 3-dataset, 10-class build.

---

## 1. Objective

Produce a single clean, training-ready vehicle-detection dataset of Dhaka
traffic, reconciled from **three** public sources that use **two different file
formats** and **three different class vocabularies** — and reshape the label
space so it is (a) balanced enough to train and (b) meaningful for a *congestion*
task rather than fine-grained vehicle identification.

---

## 2. Source datasets

| # | Source | Format | Images | Objects | Raw classes |
|---|--------|--------|-------:|--------:|------------:|
| 1 | trafic_data (DhakaAI-derived) | YOLO `.txt` | 3,004 | 24,348 | 21 |
| 2 | Poribohon-BD (mixed subset) | Pascal VOC `.xml` | 101 | ~960 | up to 15 |
| 3 | ODS_New (BNVD-derived) | YOLO `.txt` (flat) | 17,326 | 81,542 | 17 |

Incompatibilities resolved: **format** (YOLO vs VOC pixel-corner XML) and
**taxonomy** (e.g. `three wheelers -CNG-` vs `cng`; `motorbike` vs `bike`;
`Leguna` vs `human hauler`).

---

## 3. Inspection findings (before cleaning)

- **trafic_data**: structurally clean (3,004/3,004 pairing, 0 malformed, 2 empty
  labels) but **severely imbalanced** — car 5,474 vs garbagevan 3,
  **imbalance ratio 1825x**. Many classes (garbagevan 3, policecar 32,
  scooter 38, army vehicle 43, taxi 60) far below trainable thresholds.
- **Poribohon**: VOC XML; ~100 dense "mixed" multi-vehicle images; contained
  water vehicles (boat, launch) irrelevant to road traffic; 1 image–annotation
  count mismatch (orphan).
- **ODS_New**: large (17,326 images); rich local classes including Leguna,
  Easybike, Bhotbhoti, PowerTiller, Pedestrian; 20 annotations with bounding-box
  coordinates marginally exceeding image bounds (manual-annotation artifact).

---

## 4. Design decision: a congestion-relevant 10-class taxonomy

Rather than keep dozens of fragile fine-grained classes (or delete rare ones),
all raw classes were **consolidated** into 10 super-classes grouped primarily by
*traffic-flow behaviour* (size, speed, road footprint, propulsion) — the
properties that affect a congestion index. **No road vehicle was discarded**;
rare types were absorbed. Only non-road vehicles (boats, launches, horse-carts,
tractors) were dropped.

### Final classes
`0 car · 1 bus_truck · 2 rickshaw · 3 cng · 4 motorbike · 5 bicycle ·
6 human_hauler · 7 pedestrian · 8 paddlefoot_transportation · 9 auto_transportation`

### Unified mapping (all three sources)

| Unified class | Raw labels mapped in |
|---|---|
| car | car, taxi, suv, minivan, policecar, ambulance, army vehicle, MPV |
| bus_truck | bus, minibus, truck, pickup, garbagevan |
| rickshaw | rickshaw (human-pedalled) |
| cng | three wheelers -CNG-, auto rickshaw, cng, easybike |
| motorbike | motorbike, scooter, bike |
| bicycle | bicycle |
| human_hauler | human hauler, leguna |
| pedestrian | pedestrian |
| paddlefoot_transportation | van, shoppingvan, wheelbarrow |
| auto_transportation | powertiller, bhotbhoti |
| **dropped (non-road)** | boat, launch, horse-cart, tractor |

**Key rationale**
- `rickshaw` (pedalled) kept separate from `cng` (motorised three-wheeler):
  very different speed profiles, central to a speed-based congestion index.
- `auto rickshaw` routed to `cng` (not `rickshaw`) for the same reason.
- `human_hauler` (Leguna) kept as its own class: distinctive stop-start local
  vehicle supporting the "heterogeneous local traffic" thesis.
- `pedestrian` retained as a class: pedestrians materially affect Dhaka
  congestion (crossings, jaywalking) and ODS_New provides 10,581 examples.
- Two custom groupings (paddlefoot_transportation, auto_transportation) collect
  van-like and rural/non-standard engine vehicles respectively.

---

## 5. Transformations applied

A single master build script (`06_build_unified_dataset.py`) rebuilds everything
from the **untouched raw sources** in one consistent pass:

1. **trafic_data (YOLO):** rewrote class ids 21-scheme -> 10-scheme via the
   universal name map.
2. **Poribohon (VOC -> YOLO):** parsed XML; converted pixel corners to normalized
   YOLO boxes
   `xc=(xmin+xmax)/2/W, yc=(ymin+ymax)/2/H, w=(xmax-xmin)/W, h=(ymax-ymin)/H`;
   clamped out-of-bounds boxes; dropped degenerate boxes; sanitized filenames
   with spaces/parentheses (`mixed (1)` -> `mixed_1`).
3. **ODS_New (flat YOLO):** remapped 17-scheme -> 10-scheme; split 85/15
   train/valid.
4. **Merge:** combined all into `final/` with source-prefixed filenames
   (`td_`, `pb_`, `od_`) to prevent collisions; fixed random seed (42) for
   reproducible splits; wrote `data.yaml` (nc=10). Unknown class names are
   reported, never silently mishandled.

### Post-merge validation & repair
Validation (`08_validate_labels.py`) of the merged set found **20 rows with
bounding-box coordinates marginally exceeding [0,1]** (manual-annotation
artifact in ODS_New). These caused a CUDA device-side assert at train time.
A repair pass (`09_fix_label_coords.py`) clamped all box corners to valid bounds
and dropped any degenerate boxes; re-validation confirmed 0 out-of-range rows.

